      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we can use the fact that the probability of two independent events occurring is the product of their individual probabilities. The probability it will rain on Saturday is 60%, or 0.6, and the probability it will rain on Sunday is 25%, or 0.25. Since the events are independent, we can multiply these probabilities together to find the probability of both events happening.\n\nFirst, let's express the probabilities in decimal form:\n- Probability of rain on Saturday, \\( P(S) = 0.6 \\)\n- Probability of rain on Sunday, \\( P(U) = 0.25 \\)\n\nThe probability that it will rain on both days, \\( P(S \\cap U) \\), is calculated as:\n\\[ P(S \\cap U) = P(S) \\times P(U) = 0.6 \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nNow, let's perform the multiplication:\n\\[ P(S \\cap U) = 0.6 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we convert 0.15 to a percentage by multiplying by 100:\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is:\n\\[ \\boxed{15\\%} \\]",
